{
  "image_name": "02a8ff95e8707996d78854dffa9d0835.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how microbe-based electrochemical devices are improved and then applied in practical biosystems. At the top left, it shows that bare electrodes give weak electron transfer from microbial cells, limiting performance. Two broad engineering strategies are illustrated to solve this: changing electrode shape into more three-dimensional structures that hold more cells and improve contact, and modifying the surface with conductive polymers or nanoparticles to promote electron flow.\n\nThe top right focuses on the cell–electrode interface. Without protection, cells at the surface can leak, escape, or be damaged by toxins and acidic conditions. Encapsulation is presented as a way to improve environmental tolerance while maintaining electrical communication, either by embedding cells in a bulk hydrogel or by surrounding them with a thin coating.\n\nThe bottom section shows device fabrication and use cases. One example is a sediment–water microbial fuel cell powering an alarm system through a capacitor. Other examples include paper-based and painted wearable sensors on skin, a portable readout device, and an ingestible gut capsule that appears to transmit signals remotely. Overall, the figure links electrode/interface design with more robust and deployable bioelectronic devices.",
